## Supported Database Engines

### 1. MySQL
- **Versions:** 5.7, 8.0
- **Use Cases:** Web applications, e-commerce, CMS
- **Port:** 3306
- **Best for:** Open-source applications, cost-effective deployments

```bash
# Create MySQL RDS instance
aws rds create-db-instance \
  --db-instance-identifier mysql-prod \
  --db-instance-class db.t3.micro \
  --engine mysql \
  --engine-version 8.0.35 \
  --master-username admin \
  --master-user-password MyPassword123! \
  --allocated-storage 100 \
  --storage-type gp3 \
  --storage-iops 3000 \
  --db-subnet-group-name my-db-subnet \
  --publicly-accessible false
```

### 2. PostgreSQL
- **Versions:** 12, 13, 14, 15, 16
- **Use Cases:** Enterprise applications, data warehousing, geospatial data
- **Port:** 5432
- **Best for:** Advanced features, JSON support, PostGIS extension

```bash
# Create PostgreSQL RDS instance
aws rds create-db-instance \
  --db-instance-identifier postgres-prod \
  --db-instance-class db.t3.small \
  --engine postgres \
  --engine-version 16.1 \
  --master-username postgres \
  --master-user-password MyPassword123! \
  --allocated-storage 100 \
  --db-subnet-group-name my-db-subnet
```

### 3. MariaDB
- **Versions:** 10.6, 11.0
- **Use Cases:** MySQL-compatible, improved performance
- **Port:** 3306
- **Best for:** MySQL users wanting enhanced features

### 4. Oracle Database
- **Versions:** 19c, 21c
- **Use Cases:** Enterprise applications, legacy systems
- **Port:** 1521
- **Best for:** Enterprise deployments with complex requirements

### 5. Microsoft SQL Server
- **Versions:** 2019, 2022
- **Use Cases:** .NET applications, Windows-integrated systems
- **Port:** 1433
- **Best for:** Microsoft-centric environments

### 6. Amazon Aurora
- **MySQL-compatible or PostgreSQL-compatible**
- **Best for:** High-performance, scalable databases
- **Key Features:** Distributed storage, automatic scaling, pay-per-second

